! mcp_bootstrap_p136.strap -- the CONSOLE-MESSAGE scan and the
! PREPARE-UNIT / PP dispatcher, printed page 136.
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.143, header READ and not assumed:
! "TIME CLOCK 00054041  PUNID 11D11MCP   CARD ID EE000056
!  DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER  136", and the column-heading
! row's own "LOCATION 216661" names the page's first word.
!
! FIFTY-SIX PRINTED LINES, FIFTY-FIVE OF WHICH EMIT BITS.  Line 7 is
! a continuation COMMENT CARD ("-INTO P OUT FOR G TRANF"): it carries
! no LOCATION and no BINARY OUTPUT.  Everything else is code -- no
! DRZ, no XW, no SYN, no reservation.
!
! >>> FIFTY-FIVE CARDS, NOT FIFTY-SIX: LINES 1 AND 2 SHARE ONE. <<<
! The card-serial column runs EE000056 (line 1) to EE000110 (line 56),
! which is 55 serials for 56 lines, and the duplicate is at the top:
! lines 1 and 2 BOTH print EE000056, read at 1200 dpi.  That card was
! punched "SIC,G KONEC ; B,G EDIT" -- a semicolon double-statement
! card, the same shape printed p.127 found at its line 56.  Counted
! off the serial column, nothing fell off the top or the bottom of
! the render.  (The volume survey OCR'd only lines 2..42 of this page
! and reported "29 rows, 216661.4C..216717.CC"; that is a window, not
! the page.  See the agreement note at the bottom.)
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE ORIGIN IS READ TWICE, AND THE FORWARD JOIN IS WITNESSED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> THE PAGE PRINTS NO SLC. <<<  216661.00 is printed twice -- as
! line 1's own LOCATION and, as the word, in the heading row's
! "LOCATION 216661" field -- so the origin below is a READING of the
! page.  What it is NOT is a test of contiguity with printed p.135:
! a hard SLC asserts its value rather than checking it
! (docs/transcription_workflow.md, "A HARD SLC CANNOT DISAGREE WITH
! THE ARRIVING COUNTER").  THE BACKWARD JOIN IS NOT CHECKED HERE.
! The volume survey OCR'd printed p.135's LOCATION column only as
! far as its line 42 (216647.00), but the volume listing OCR carries
! a row further down that page -- "50* 216654.40  216745.77 80
! 216722.34 00  BZB,P TEMBF+2.63,P PPRET" -- so p.135 line 50 is at
! 216654.40 with six lines below it.  Six half-to-full words from
! 216654.40 reaches 216661.00 comfortably, so the join is
! CONSISTENT; it is not MEASURED, because a hard SLC asserts rather
! than checks.  When p.135 lands, compare by hand.
!
! THE FORWARD JOIN HAS THREE WITNESSES AND THEY ALL AGREE.
! Line 56 is a full-word connective at 216730.00, so it occupies
! 216730.00 and 216730.40 and the counter leaves at 216731.00.
!   (a) The survey OCR of printed p.137 (PDF p.144) reads its line 2
!       at 216732.00 and its line 3 at 216732.40 and does not read
!       its line 1 -- which therefore sits at 216731.00, exactly
!       where this page's counter arrives.
!   (b) Line 53 branches to P MP LEV at 216732.40, and the volume
!       OCR carries that symbol's DEFINING card there:
!       "3# 216732.4C 215361.CC 30  P MP LEV  LV,$0,S IC BU".
!       That is printed p.137 line 3.
!   (c) Line 47 branches to G ERROR at 216733.40, and the volume OCR
!       carries ITS defining card there too:
!       "# 216733.40  21703?.C3 01  G ERROR  LVI,$1,...".
!       That is printed p.137 line 6, and the survey's own location
!       column reads 216733.40 on that line.
! So nothing fell off the bottom of this page.
!
! TAIL SCOPE.  printed p.99 line 11's "SLC,(8)215000." moved the
! origin here and pp.99+ are OUTSIDE the TAIL,(2)G block that printed
! p.2 opened and printed p.98 line 37 closed with "UNTAIL,(2)".
! Names defined here may legitimately repeat names from pp.2-98 at
! other addresses; none of this page's eight does.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE CONTAINS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Two routines laid end to end.
!
! Lines 1-25   the tail of the CONSOLE MESSAGE handler that begins on
!              an earlier page.  Line 1 saves the IC into G KONEC and
!              line 2 jumps into G EDIT (same card).  Lines 3-8
!              round the character count to a whole number of words
!              ("- IS NO. OF CHARS. DIVISIBLE BY 8") and plant it in
!              P OUT for G TRANF.  Lines 9-25 are the SCAN LOOP:
!              G ZPET loads the next character of the typed message
!              with progressive indexing (line 12's "L(V+I)"), and
!              four KFI compares test it against carriage return
!              (2)1111 1101, tab (2)1111 1011, blank (2)0 and the
!              letter P (2)0100 1011 -- forcing upper case with
!              C1111(BU,1) first.  A P sends control to P PP RET;
!              anything else falls into P MC RET.
! Lines 26-31  P MC RET, the MCP-side return: index JC IOD off
!              S BAMCP, pick up the activated file-area address into
!              S FI AAC, and Transmit the edited message out of
!              G MISTO into P MCP BF.
! Lines 32-46  P MAIN / P MAIN1 / P EX1, the PREPARE-UNIT entry.
!              Save STIC into S INT AD, move 3 words of P TEM BF into
!              P MCP BF, clear S UNIT in the CST ("-SET MCP UNIT(=0)
!              IN CST"), unselect the PP unit and select the prepare
!              unit (lines 36-37, two CM connectives on S SEL one
!              word apart), turn on the console-signal bit, then test
!              S UN SUP: if suppressed, skip to JPEX2; otherwise copy
!              five bits of SIO IND and merge them with C0111.
!              Line 45 sets $1 to K NORM and line 46 branches to
!              K Q IN.
! Lines 47-56  P PP RET, the PP-side return: check that the console
!              is assigned to the PP ("-IS CNSL ASSIGNED TO PP",
!              branching to G ERROR if not), walk S UN A twice to
!              reach the PP's unit area, fetch S FI AAC, Transmit 13
!              words of P TEM BF into P PP BF, and -- if S L says so
!              -- hand off to P MP LEV.  P COM (line 55) stores STIC
!              "-INTO INTER ADDR SLOT OF PPSFAT".
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE EIGHT NAMES THIS PAGE DEFINES
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
!   G ZPET   216666.40   P MC RET 216703.00   P MAIN  216706.40
!   P MAIN1  216707.00   P EX1    216712.40   JPEX2   216720.40
!   P PP RET 216722.00   P COM    216727.40
!
! FIVE OF THE EIGHT HAVE AN INDEPENDENT OFF-PAGE WITNESS in the
! volume OCR -- a reference printed on a page nobody here has opened,
! at the address this page gives it.  That is a second reading of the
! SPELLING, which no !CHECK can supply.  In each case the half word
! is recovered the same way: bit 18 is the TOP bit of the printed
! octal pair, so 0o74 and 0o50 mean .40 and 0o34 and 0o10 mean .00.
!   P PP RET 216722.00  printed p.135 line 50, "216654.40
!                       216745.77 80  216722.34 00
!                       BZB,P TEMBF+2.63,P PPRET".
!   P MAIN   216706.40  printed p.137, "216740.40 *  215001.04 80
!                       216706.74 00  BZB,S L,P MAIN" -- and note
!                       that p.137 tests the SAME bit S L that line
!                       53 here tests.
!   P MAIN1  216707.00  printed p.137, "216742.00  216707.10 00
!                       B,PMAIN1".
!   P EX1    216712.40  printed p.137 line 2, "216732.00
!                       216712.50 00  B,PEX1".
!   P COM    216727.40  printed p.137, "216733.00  216727.50 00
!                       B,P COM".  The NAME column there reads
!                       "P COM" and settles the two round glyphs
!                       that this page's own ink does not.
! THE THREE WITH NO OFF-PAGE WITNESS are G ZPET, P MC RET and JPEX2.
! Each is still referenced from a SECOND row on this page and
! compiles bit-exactly at every use (G ZPET from lines 14, 16 and 18,
! P MC RET from lines 11 and 21, JPEX2 from line 40), so their
! LOCATIONS are safe; only their SPELLINGS rest on ink.
!
! THOSE FOUR p.137 ROWS ARE ALSO FOUR MORE FORWARD-JOIN WITNESSES:
! p.137 branches back into the middle of THIS page at four distinct
! addresses, all of which this deck's !SYM block puts where p.137
! expects them.  And three of them are a plain "B" printing byte 00,
! which is the independent corroboration of this page's own lines 2
! and 46 discussed below.
!
! "P MC RET" -- the glyph after the M is round and open.  Nothing on
! the page disambiguates it; what argues for C is the sense (this is
! the MCP-side return, the twin of the P PP RET three lines up, and
! line 31 spells the MCP buffer "P MCP BF" on this same page) and
! both volume-OCR readings of this page's own rows, "P MC RET" and
! "PMCRET".  Recorded as a reading, not a proof.
!
! "G ZPET" / "GZPET" and "P PP RET" / "P PPRET" are the same symbol
! printed with and without blanks, which is STRAP ignoring blanks in
! a name; the deck writes the blankless form throughout.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE C/0 QUESTION IN THE BYTE COLUMN, SETTLED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> BOTH BYTES WERE READ WRONG ON THE FIRST PASS AND THE
! CORRECTION IS RECORDED HERE RATHER THAN SILENTLY APPLIED. <<<  At
! 1800 dpi in a tight crop both came back "C0"; the re-read was done
! against line 4's "C2" IN THE SAME COLUMN -- an independent
! reference glyph on the page, not the assembler's answer.
!
! Lines 2 and 46 are plain branches and their bytes matter, because
! "C0" and "00" are one ink-dropout apart and this drum opens round
! glyphs constantly -- line 1's own octal field prints ".00" as
! ".CC", and line 34's prints ".06" as ".C6".  Three things settle
! them as 00:
!   (a) SHAPE, compared IN THE SAME COLUMN.  At 1800 dpi line 4's
!       "C2" has a wide open right side; the first glyph of lines 2
!       and 46 is a closed oval with at most a pinhole nick.  The
!       asymmetry runs one way -- dropout opens a closed glyph and
!       never closes an open one -- so a glyph this closed is a 0.
!   (b) THE OCTAL FIELD IS ".10" ON BOTH ROWS, which is the
!       unconditional-branch code; every conditional branch on this
!       page prints a different octal pair (BAE/BXE/BRZ .36/.32/.34
!       with C2, BZAE .36 with C0).  There is no branch that prints
!       ".10" beside a C0.
!   (c) AN IDENTICAL HALF WORD IS ALREADY LANDED.  printed p.126
!       line 56 is the same instruction to the same word --
!       "216170.40  215762.10 00  B,K ? IN" -- and its !CHECK passes.
!       THE VOLUME OCR READS THAT ROW AS "215762.1C CO", i.e. the OCR
!       turns this exact byte into C0 where the page prints 00.  So
!       the OCR's "C0" on this page's line 46 is the OCR's own known
!       failure and is not evidence.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! LINE 34's BYTE IS A0, NOT AC
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! At 1800 dpi line 34's second half word reads "216775.C6 AC".  The
! ".C6" is certainly ".06" (a C cannot occur in an octal field), so
! this row drops ink on round glyphs before it reaches the byte --
! but that is NOT a licence to convert the byte (ink varies per
! CHARACTER).  What settles it is WHAT PRODUCED IT: the low nibble of
! a Transmit's second half word is i2, the index of the second
! operand, and "TI,3,P TEM BF,P MCP BF" has no index on either
! operand, so it is 0.  Line 52 is the same instruction with a
! different count ("TI,13,P TEM BF,P PP BF") and prints a CLOSED 0 in
! that position.  The two rows differ only in the octal field,
! 216775.06 against 216760.32, which is the count 3 against 13 times
! two -- exactly the field this page uses for a register everywhere
! else.  Line 35's "003000.CC FC" is 003000.00 F0 for the same
! reason: printed p.128 line 2's landed row "000004.26 8D
! 001000.00 F0" is the same connective shape with no second-operand
! index, and line 56 HERE prints the same F0 with a closed 0.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EXTERNALS.  WHAT IS READ AND WHAT IS CONVENTION
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> AN ADDRESS BACK-DERIVED FROM A PRINTED BRANCH, LVI OR SIC IS
! PINNED ONLY TO THE HALF WORD. <<<  Bit offsets 0-31 all encode
! identically; only bit 18, the half-word select, shows.  Writing
! ".0" is a CONVENTION and no !CHECK on this page can test the bits
! below it (docs/transcription_workflow.md, "WHAT THE ORACLE CANNOT
! SEE", item 1).  The half word ITSELF is checked and does catch
! errors, which is why the ".40" values below are written as read.
!
! GKONEC  217063.00  NEW.  Line 1 prints "217063.00 80" and SIC
!                    prints its operand's address WITH the half word
!                    (printed p.128 line 40's landed "SIC,K ST CS"
!                    prints 216272.40), so THE HALF WORD is read --
!                    but only the half word.  The bit offset inside
!                    it is the usual convention and no check here
!                    can see it.  The volume OCR carries the
!                    DEFINING card -- "217063.00  217063.10 00
!                    G KONEC  B,$" -- and one more reference,
!                    "216654.00  217063.36 C2  BAE,G KONEC  -YES-OUT".
GKONEC  SYN, (8)217063.0
! GEDIT   217045.00  NEW.  Line 2 prints "217045.10 00"; bit 18 of
!                    0o10 is clear, so the half word is .00.  The
!                    volume OCR carries the defining card,
!                    "217045.00  000024.22 00  G EDIT  Z,34
!                    -INITIALIZE", and the row below it,
!                    "217045.40 ... LVI,$4,P TEMBF+3.0".
GEDIT   SYN, (8)217045.0
! GMISTO  217012.00  NEW.  Line 9 prints "217012.13 01" (LVI,$5, so
!                    0o13 = 5*2+1 and the offset is 0) and line 31's
!                    first half word prints "217012.00 80" -- a
!                    Transmit source, where .00 is read.  The volume
!                    OCR carries four more references at word 217012
!                    on two other pages.
GMISTO  SYN, (8)217012.0
! POUT    217106.40  NEW, AND FULLY OBSERVED.  Line 8 is a VFL
!                    connective whose operand address is a BIT
!                    address: it prints "217106.40 80".  Line 30's
!                    "LC,$4,P OUT" prints 217106.50, which is 0o40
!                    plus 0o10 = register 4 times two -- the same
!                    .40 arrived at a second way.  The volume OCR
!                    carries the defining card, "217106.40
!                    000000.00+  P OUT  VF, 0.0".
POUT    SYN, (8)217106.40
! PTEMBF  216743.00  NEW.  Lines 34 and 52 print "216743.00 80".
!                    The volume OCR carries the DEFINING card --
!                    "216743.00 *  000015.00  P TEM BF  DRZ(BU,64),
!                    (13)" -- a thirteen-word buffer, which is
!                    exactly the count line 52 transmits.
PTEMBF  SYN, (8)216743.0
! PMCPBF  216775.00  NEW.  Line 34 prints "216775.06 A0" and line 31
!                    prints "P MCP BF+3.0" as 217000.10 -- and
!                    0o216775 + 3 = 0o217000, so the WORD is
!                    confirmed twice by arithmetic.  The volume OCR
!                    adds "217103.40  216775.05 01  P MCP
!                    LVI,$2,P MCP BF  -SET TO TRANSFER FROM MCP BF".
PMCPBF  SYN, (8)216775.0
! PPPBF   216760.00  NEW.  Line 52 prints "216760.32 A0".  The
!                    volume OCR carries the defining card,
!                    "216760.00  000015.00  P PP BF  DRZ(BU,64),
!                    (13)", and a reference "217073.40  216760.05 01
!                    LVI,$2,PPPBF  -PP-SET TO TRANSFER FROM PP BF".
PPPBF   SYN, (8)216760.0
! GERROR  216733.40  NEW.  Line 47's branch half prints
!                    "216733.74 02"; bit 18 of 0o74 is SET, so the
!                    target is the .40 half word -- that bit IS
!                    observable and IS checked.  The volume OCR
!                    carries the defining card at 216733.40 with
!                    "G ERROR" in the NAME column.
GERROR  SYN, (8)216733.40
! PMPLEV  216732.40  NEW.  Line 53 prints "216732.74 02", same
!                    reasoning, .40 observed.  The volume OCR's
!                    defining card is "216732.40  215361.00 30
!                    P MP LEV  LV,$0,S IC BU".
PMPLEV  SYN, (8)216732.40
! KNORM   215776.00  LANDED at this value by decks/mcp_bootstrap_
!                    p128.strap.  Line 45 prints "215776.03 01",
!                    identical to p.128's line 33/35 references.
KNORM   SYN, (8)215776.0
! KQIN    215762.00  LANDED by decks/mcp_bootstrap_p123.strap (which
!                    transcribes its DEFINING card, "215762.00
!                    215021.23 01  K Q IN  LVI,9,S PROG S") and by
!                    p128.  Line 46 here prints "215762.10 00".
!                    >>> THE THIRD GLYPH IS Q. <<<  decks/mcp_
!                    bootstrap_p126.strap spells the same word
!                    K G IN, so this is a live three-to-one dispute
!                    and this page is a fourth, independent reading
!                    of the glyph.  At 1800 dpi line 46's glyph is a
!                    round bowl cut at the LOWER RIGHT by a diagonal
!                    stroke and has NO horizontal bar -- set beside
!                    the G of "S UN ASG" one row below in the same
!                    column band, which does have the bar, they are
!                    plainly different letters.  This deck follows
!                    p123/p128.
KQIN    SYN, (8)215762.0
! STIC    215265.00  LANDED by p110, p111, p112, p118 and p128, and
!                    named in C22-6728's prose ("the saved IC is
!                    stored in STIC"), which is what settles the
!                    final letter as C.  Lines 32 and 54 print
!                    "215265.00 30".
STIC    SYN, (8)215265.0
! SBAMCP  215003.00  LANDED, decks/mcp_bootstrap_p103.strap !SYM line
!                    31 ("S BAMCP VF,S MCP").  Line 27 prints
!                    "215003.02 B0" -- 0o02 is register 1 times two,
!                    so the word is read and the offset is 0.
SBAMCP  SYN, (8)215003.0
! SCOMRG  215001.00  LANDED, decks/mcp_bootstrap_p103.strap line 8
!                    ("S COM RG XW,(8)520000.").  Carried only so
!                    that S L below can be written the way p.103
!                    writes it.
SCOMRG  SYN, (8)215001.0
! SL      215001.04  LANDED, p.103 line 14, "S L SYN(BU,1),
!                    S COM RG + 0.04".  FULLY OBSERVED HERE: line 53
!                    is a Branch on Bit, so the printed "215001.04"
!                    is a BIT address and bit 4 is read, not
!                    conventional.
SL      SYN(BU,1), SCOMRG+0.04
! JCIOD   000010.00  >>> NEW, AND THE ONE SYMBOL WITH NO WITNESS
!                    ANYWHERE. <<<  Line 26 is its only use in
!                    either OCR volume: "LVI,$1, JC IOD" printing
!                    "000010.03 01", where 0o03 is register 1 times
!                    two plus one -- the whole printed octal pair is
!                    consumed by the register and the half-word
!                    select, so the WORD 0o10 (8) is read and the
!                    bit offset inside the half word is convention,
!                    not a reading.  Line 27 then adds S BAMCP, the base of
!                    the MCP's I/O area, so JC IOD is an OFFSET
!                    within that area and a small word count is what
!                    it ought to be.  The SPELLING rests entirely on
!                    this page's ink (the J and the D are
!                    unambiguous -- the D has the straight vertical
!                    left stem -- and the two round glyphs are read
!                    as 0 and O from the "IOD" that printed pp.84
!                    and 102 use throughout).  The MODE of its
!                    defining card is unknown; SYN with no data
!                    description is written because line 26 is an
!                    immediate-index operation and takes only the
!                    value.
JCIOD   SYN, (8)10.0
! The remaining nine are VFL FIELD DEFINITIONS, not addresses.  Each
! is copied VERBATIM from the landed deck that defines it, because
! the SYN's mode and length are part of what the referencing rows
! compile -- and because a field symbol's value IS fully observable
! here: it lands in the address field as a bit address.
! SUNAA/SUNA  0.0   LANDED, decks/mcp_bootstrap_p99.strap lines
!                   19-20.  p.99 defines S UN A as a second name for
!                   S UN A A on purpose.  Lines 48 and 50 print
!                   000000.26 3A and 000000.30 3B -- registers 11
!                   and 12 times two, with bit offset 0 read.
SUNAA   SYN(BU,25), 0.0
SUNA    SYN, SUNAA
! SUNASG  0.29  LANDED, p.99 line 27 "-UNIT ASSIGNMENT".  Line 47
!               prints 000001.35 for "S UN ASG+1.0": 0o35 = 29, and
!               a Branch on Bit prints the BIT ADDRESS directly
!               (printed p.124 line 47's landed "BB,SUNASG($11),
!               KSUPP" prints 000000.35).  Fully observed.
SUNASG  SYN(BU,1), 0.29
! SUNSUP  0.30  LANDED, p.99 line 28 "-UNIT SUPPRESSION".
!               >>> THIS PAGE PRINTS THE NAME AS "SUNSLP". <<<  The
!               glyph before the P has a left stem and a bottom bar
!               and reads L at 1800 dpi; a worn U loses its right
!               stem and becomes exactly that.  THE ADDRESS SETTLES
!               IT: line 40 prints 000000.36 = bit 30, which is
!               S UN SUP's bit in p.99's unit-area table, and there
!               is no S UN SLP anywhere in that table or in either
!               OCR volume.  p.99's own comment reads SUPPRESSION.
!               Spelled SUNSUP here, as p.99 spells it.
SUNSUP  SYN(BU,1), 0.30
! SSEL    0.39  LANDED, p.99 line 32 "-SELECT FILE STATUS".  Lines
!               36 and 37 print 000001.47 and 000000.47 -- one word
!               apart, 0o47 = 39 both times.  Observed.
SSEL    SYN(BU,1), 0.39
! SCNSSG  0.47  LANDED, p.99 line 35 "-CONSOLE SIGNAL".  Line 38
!               prints 000000.57 = 47.  Observed, and the comment
!               "-TURN ON PROPER S CNS SG BIT" names it in full.
SCNSSG  SYN(BU,1), 0.47
! SUNIT   0.56  LANDED, p.99 line 39, and already carried at this
!               value by the p122 and p125 decks.  Lines 35 and 56
!               print 000000.70 and 000000.72 ("S UNIT+.2"), 0o70 =
!               56.  Observed twice.
SUNIT   SYN(BU,3), 0.56
! SFIAAC  3.0   LANDED, decks/mcp_bootstrap_p101.strap line 13.
!               Lines 29 and 51 print 000003.33 and 000003.32 -- SV
!               and LV of register 13, 26 and 27 -- so the WORD 3 is
!               read off the page.
SFIAAC  SYN(BU,18), 3.0
! SIOIND  1.09  LANDED, decks/mcp_bootstrap_p102.strap line 7 "-IO
!               INDICATORS".  >>> LINE 41 IS A ROW ANOTHER PAGE
!               ALREADY PRINTS. <<<  printed p.123 line 2 is the same
!               statement and the same two half words, "000001.11 8D
!               005071.60 50", landed and passing in decks/mcp_
!               bootstrap_p123.strap.  That is an exact off-page
!               confirmation of this row's bits.
SIOIND  SYN(BU,5), 1.09
! SINTAD  1.32  LANDED, p.102 line 8 "-INTERRUPTED ADDRESS".  Lines
!               33 and 55 print 000001.41 3D, identical to printed
!               p.123 line 36's landed "SV,0,S INT AD($13)".
SINTAD  SYN(BU,19), 1.32
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! TWO ASSEMBLER GAPS.  ONE WAS FIXED WHILE THIS PAGE WAS BEING READ
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> GAP 1, A RADIX LITERAL WITH A BLANK INSIDE IT.  FOUND HERE
! INDEPENDENTLY, FIXED UPSTREAM AT 36290f2, AND THESE FOUR ROWS ARE
! FOUR MORE WITNESSES FOR THAT FIX. <<<  Lines 13, 15, 20 and 24
! print their immediate as "(2)1111 1101", "(2)1111 1011" and
! "(2)0100 1011" -- one blank grouping the two nibbles, read at 1800
! dpi and quite unambiguous.  STRAP ignores blanks (this repository
! has already accepted "VF,S ASPUT" and "SIC,S T IC"), and IBM's
! assembler puts the literal left-justified in the 18-bit address
! field:
!
!     (2)1111 1101  ->  772000   = 0b11111101 << 10
!     (2)1111 1011  ->  766000
!     (2)0100 1011  ->  226000
!     (2)0          ->  000000
!
! As first transcribed, these four rows FAILED, and they were left
! failing on purpose rather than written "(2)11111101" to make them
! pass.  strap_asm was not rejecting the printed form -- it was
! SILENTLY COMPILING A DIFFERENT WORD, which is the worse failure:
! tokenize_expr read the blank as a term separator, so "(2)1111 1101"
! became (2)1111 PLUS 1101 and emitted 270000 for 772000, 004000 for
! 766000 and 756000 for 226000, with no error and no warning.
! printed p.135 line 53 ("KFI(BU,8),(2)1111 1110") hit the same
! defect in the same hour and it was fixed in strap_asm's
! tokenize_expr at commit 36290f2.  AFTER THAT FIX ALL FOUR ROWS
! HERE PASS, and they are an independent corroboration on three
! literals p.135 does not use.  Line 17's "(2)0" has no blank and
! passed throughout, which is what localised the defect.
!
! >>> GAP 2 (SUBSTITUTED, BECAUSE IT ABORTS THE ASSEMBLY).  A
! CONNECTIVE WITH NO ADDRESS FIELD AT ALL. <<<  Lines 19 and 23 print
! "C1111(BU,1)" and nothing else -- no operand, no comma, just the
! data description, with the comment "-FORCE UPPER CASE CHARACTER"
! three inches away.  IBM's assembler read that as address zero with
! index zero and printed "000000.00 80  001000.36 70".  strap_asm
! raises "C1111 needs an address field", which is an AssemblerError
! and stops the whole deck, so leaving it as printed would check
! nothing at all.  The deck therefore writes "C1111(BU,1)",
! which assembles BIT-IDENTICALLY to the printed row (verified: both
! half words match), and the !CHECK notes carry the printed form.
! This is a SUBSTITUTION and it is flagged; the gap is real.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT IS RECORDED AND NOT EXPLAINED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! A BARE "*" AFTER THE LOCATION on lines 3, 20, 36 and 52 --
! "216662.00 *", "216676.00 *", "216711.40 *", "216725.00 *".  The
! volume survey's independent OCR of the LOCATION column marks lines
! 3, 20 and 36 the same way, so the marks are on the page and not in
! the render.  printed pp.3, 37, 103, 120 and 128 carry the same mark
! and it was left unexplained on each.  It is not a DRZ marker (there
! is no DRZ here) and not a "$-relative operand" marker (lines 4 and
! 6 use $ and print no star, while line 3 does -- though line 3 also
! uses $4).  It changes no compiled bit.
!
! THE IBM TYPO IS TRANSCRIBED AS PRINTED.  Line 7's comment reads
! "-INTO P OUT FOR G TRANF", i.e. TRANsFer with the S dropped; it is
! the listing's own and G TRANF is presumably a routine name.
! Line 50's comment prints "-SELECT PPS UATA"; the word is DATA and
! the D has lost its top-left corner, the ordinary C/D failure of
! this drum read in the other direction.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! COVERAGE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! ALL FIFTY-FIVE EMITTING ROWS CARRY A !CHECK and all eight symbols a
! !SYM, and as of strap_asm 36290f2 ALL FIFTY-FIVE PASS.  Line 7 has
! no !CHECK because it is a comment card and emits nothing.  Lines
! 13, 15, 20 and 24 failed when first transcribed and were left
! failing; see GAP 1 above.  Lines 19 and 23 pass on a flagged
! SUBSTITUTION, not on the printed form; see GAP 2.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------

        SLC, (8)216661.0

        SIC,GKONEC                      ! line  1
        B,GEDIT                         ! line  2  -EDIT MESSAGE
        CT0011(BU,3),$4+.43             ! line  3  - IS NO. OF CHARS. DIVISIBLE BY 8
        BRZ,$+1.0                       ! line  4
        C+I,$4,8.0                      ! line  5
        L(BU,15),$4+.28,71              ! line  6  -PUT NO. OF FULL WDS. (TO BE TRANSFERRED)
!                                       ! line  7  -INTO P OUT FOR G TRANF   (comment card)
        CM0101(BU,25),POUT,64           ! line  8
        LVI,$5,GMISTO                   ! line  9
GZPET   KCI,$4,0                        ! line 10
        BXE,PMCRET                      ! line 11
        L(V+I)(BU,8),.8($5)             ! line 12  -LOAD CHARACTER AT TYPED MESSAGE-
        KFI(BU,8),(2)1111 1101          ! line 13  -IS IT CARRIAGE RETURN CODE
        BAE,GZPET                       ! line 14
        KFI(BU,8),(2)1111 1011          ! line 15  -IS IT A TAB CODE
        BAE,GZPET                       ! line 16
        KFI(BU,8),(2)0                  ! line 17  -IS IT A BLANK CODE
        BAE,GZPET                       ! line 18
        C1111(BU,1)                 ! line 19  -FORCE UPPER CASE CHARACTER  (printed "C1111(BU,1)")
        KFI(BU,8),(2)0100 1011          ! line 20  -IS IT A P CHARACTER
        BZAE,PMCRET                     ! line 21
        L(BU,8),0($5)                   ! line 22  -LOAD NEXT CHARACTER
        C1111(BU,1)                 ! line 23  -FORCE UPPER CASE  (printed "C1111(BU,1)")
        KFI(BU,8),(2)0100 1011          ! line 24  -IS IT A P CHARACTER
        BAE,PPPRET                      ! line 25  -YES-GO TO PP
PMCRET  LVI,$1,JCIOD                    ! line 26
        V+,$1,SBAMCP                    ! line 27
        LV,$13,.32($1)                  ! line 28
        SV,13,SFIAAC($12)               ! line 29  -ACTIVATED FILE AREA ADDR TO UA
        LC,$4,POUT                      ! line 30
        T,$4,GMISTO,PMCPBF+3.0          ! line 31
PMAIN   LV,$0,STIC                      ! line 32
PMAIN1  SV,$0,SINTAD($13)               ! line 33
        TI,3,PTEMBF,PMCPBF              ! line 34
        CM0000(BU,3),SUNIT($10)         ! line 35  -SET MCP UNIT(=0) IN CST
        CM1111(BU,1),SSEL+1.0($11)      ! line 36  -UNSELECT PP UNIT
PEX1    CM0000(BU,1),SSEL($11)          ! line 37  -SELECT PREPARE UNIT
        CM1111(BU,1),SCNSSG($11)        ! line 38  -TURN ON PROPER S CNS SG BIT
        LCI,$7,(8)20.00                 ! line 39
        BZB,SUNSUP($11),JPEX2           ! line 40
        L(BU,5),SIOIND($13),-13         ! line 41
        C0111(BU,5),$7.37,-13           ! line 42
        LC,7,$L                         ! line 43
JPEX2   SC,7,1.0($13)                   ! line 44
        LVI,$1,KNORM                    ! line 45  -FAT
        B,KQIN                          ! line 46
PPPRET  BB,SUNASG+1.0($11),GERROR       ! line 47  -IS CNSL ASSIGNED TO PP
        LV,$11,SUNA($10)                ! line 48
        V+I,$11,1.0                     ! line 49
        LV,$12,SUNA($11)                ! line 50  -SELECT PPS DATA
        LV,13,SFIAAC($12)               ! line 51  -GET FILE AREA ACTIVATED ADDR
        TI,13,PTEMBF,PPPBF              ! line 52
        BB,SL,PMPLEV                    ! line 53
        LV,$0,STIC                      ! line 54  -INTERRUPT AT PP LEVEL PUT
PCOM    SV,$0,SINTAD($13)               ! line 55  -CONTENTS OF STIC
        CM1111(BU,1),SUNIT+.2($10)      ! line 56  -INTO INTER ADDR SLOT OF PPSFAT

